# Comparing Libraries

How much progress does each implementation make, and how long does it take?
These comparisons follow individual solvers on shared problems. The curves show
the objective gap $f(\mathbf{x}) - f^\star$ against elapsed time, so you can
compare the time needed to reach a given accuracy as well as the accuracy
ultimately reached.

The harness matches configurations where the APIs allow it. Each section
describes the remaining differences, which can change the optimization path and
the amount of work. For comparisons between algorithms within Basin, see
[comparing solvers](../solvers/).

## Levenberg–Marquardt

This case asks how two least-squares implementations handle the same small,
poorly scaled fitting problem. Basin {data.versions.basin} and
[levenberg-marquardt](https://docs.rs/levenberg-marquardt/0.15.0/levenberg_marquardt/)
{lmVersion} fit $y(t) = a\exp(bt)$ to ten exact observations.
Both start from $(a_0, b_0) = (50\,000, -0.3)$ and seek
$(a^\star, b^\star) = (100\,000, -1)$.

Both use nalgebra {data.versions.nalgebra}, the same analytic Jacobian, column
scaling, pivoted QR, and an initial scaled step-bound factor of 100.
Function-reduction, orthogonality, and scaled-radius tolerances are 30 times
`f64` machine epsilon, about $6.66 \times 10^{-15}$. Basin uses trust-region
damping and disables its absolute-gradient and unscaled-step checks. Both have a
300-residual-call budget; Basin also has a 200-iteration safety cap.

<CompetitorFigure solver="lm" problem="exp_fit" />

In this snapshot, both implementations return the exact fitting parameters and
use six residual evaluations. The reference finishes sooner, while Basin makes
one additional Jacobian evaluation. The returned-solution details distinguish
those evaluation counts from elapsed time.

The reference uses its MINPACK implementation. Internal damping searches,
acceptance rules, and numerical safeguards differ. The curve records the best
objective seen at residual evaluations, including rejected trials, so this
comparison includes algorithmic choices as well as implementation cost.

## COBYLA

For a constrained solve, objective progress needs to be read together with
feasibility. This case solves

$$
\begin{aligned}
\min_{x,y}\quad & (x-1)^2 + (y-1)^2 \\
\text{subject to}\quad & 0 \le x,y \le 2, \\
& x+y \le 1.5.
\end{aligned}
$$

Both implementations start at $(0.5, 0.5)$. The constrained optimum is
$(x^\star, y^\star) = (0.75, 0.75)$, with objective value $f^\star = 0.125$.

Basin's PRIMA implementation is compared with
[cobyla](https://docs.rs/cobyla/) {data.versions.cobyla}, derived from NLopt.
Both use an initial radius of $0.5$, a final radius of about
$7.45 \times 10^{-9}$, a 100-objective-call budget, and disabled optional
objective-change stops. A trial is eligible for the objective curve only when
its maximum constraint violation
is at most $1.49 \times 10^{-8}$.

<CompetitorFigure solver="cobyla" problem="quadratic_2d" />

Both returned points pass the independent accuracy and feasibility checks in
this snapshot. Basin uses fewer objective calls, but the reference finishes
sooner. This illustrates why evaluation counts and elapsed time answer different
questions, even on the same problem.

The first plot retains only improvements in the best feasible objective. Read
the second plot to see infeasible trials that the first plot omits. A small
negative objective gap allowed by the feasibility tolerance is displayed at the
positive floor; it does not imply an improvement over the exact constrained
optimum. Constraint counts also use different callback units: Basin records
vector callbacks including bounds, while the reference records scalar nonlinear
callbacks and excludes native bounds.

## SLSQP

This comparison uses unconstrained Rosenbrock in two dimensions, starting at
$(-1.2, 1)$, with analytic gradients. It compares Basin with
[slsqp](https://docs.rs/slsqp/1.0.2/slsqp/) {data.versions.slsqp}, a Rust
translation of NLopt 2.7.1, and native NLopt 2.9.1. The case measures the
unconstrained optimization path; it does not exercise constraint handling.

All three curves record the best objective seen at function evaluations,
include solve initialization, and end after termination. Basin uses Kraft's
composite accuracy test at $10^{-10}$. The references use an absolute
function-change tolerance of $10^{-10}$, with their other optional tolerances
disabled. Basin has a 200-iteration safety cap; the references have a
200-evaluation cap.

<CompetitorFigure solver="slsqp" problem="rosenbrock" />

The three curves follow closely in this snapshot and reach similar final
objective gaps. All returned solutions pass an independent objective and
gradient check before the harness emits results. The stopping tests and budget
units still differ, so the shared numerical tolerance does not make the
termination rules identical.

## Gradient Descent

This case compares steepest descent in Basin and
[argmin](https://argmin-rs.org/) {data.versions.argmin}. Both use a
Moré–Thuente line search on two-dimensional Rosenbrock from $(-1.2, 1)$, with a
{data.iterations}-iteration cap.

<CompetitorFigure solver="gd" problem="rosenbrock" />

Neither run reaches the optimum within the recorded budget. Basin finishes the
budget sooner and with a smaller objective gap in this snapshot. The distinct
paths matter: a shared algorithm and line-search family still allow the
implementations to take different steps. Compare the time at a common objective
gap as well as the endpoints.

## Nelder–Mead

Nelder–Mead adds an important setup choice: the initial simplex. Basin and
argmin use identical simplex coordinates and standard coefficients on
two-dimensional Rosenbrock. [gomez](https://docs.rs/gomez/)
{data.versions.gomez} and NLopt build their own simplexes with their default
coefficients from the same starting point, $(-1.2, 1)$.

Basin, argmin, and gomez have a {data.iterations}-iteration cap. NLopt has a
{data.iterations}-objective-evaluation cap and records improvements at function
evaluations rather than at iterations.

<CompetitorFigure solver="nm" problem="rosenbrock" />

Basin, argmin, and gomez reach the display floor in this snapshot, at different
times. NLopt's recorded curve ends at a larger objective gap. The Basin–argmin
pair controls the initial simplex; the other curves also reflect initialization
and configuration choices. Read them as the behavior of those configurations
on this problem.

## L-BFGS

The L-BFGS case uses the same two-dimensional Rosenbrock problem and starting
point. Basin and argmin retain ten curvature pairs and use a Moré–Thuente line
search. NLopt supplies its bundled L-BFGS implementation, whose line search is
not configurable through the harness's API.

Basin and argmin have a {data.iterations}-iteration cap, with argmin's gradient
and cost tolerances zeroed. NLopt instead has a
{data.iterations}-objective-evaluation cap. An iteration can evaluate the
objective more than once, so these limits do not prescribe equal work.

<CompetitorFigure solver="lbfgs" problem="rosenbrock" />

All three curves reach the display floor in this snapshot. Basin and NLopt do
so at similar elapsed times, ahead of argmin. This is evidence for a small,
smooth problem with cheap evaluations; it does not establish the same ordering
for higher-dimensional problems or expensive objectives. See the
[L-BFGS guide](../../docs/solvers/quasi-newton/l-bfgs/) for algorithm selection
and configuration.

## NEWUOA

This case compares two implementations of Powell's model-based derivative-free
method on five-dimensional Styblinski–Tang, starting at the origin. Basin is
based on the modernized PRIMA implementation, while NLopt uses Powell's
reference implementation.

The initial radius is $0.5$, the target final radius is $10^{-6}$, and the
interpolation set has $2n+1$ points. The harness matches these settings as
closely as the APIs allow. Basin has a 1,000-iteration safety cap and NLopt a
1,000-evaluation cap; both runs stop before those limits.

<CompetitorFigure solver="newuoa" problem="styblinski" />

Both implementations approach the known global minimum from this start, with
NLopt reaching its endpoint sooner in this snapshot. This multimodal problem
tests the model-building path from one starting point. It does not measure the
probability of finding the global minimum from arbitrary starts.

## Methodology and Reproduction

Measured {data.generatedAt} on {data.env.cpu} ({data.env.os}/{data.env.arch}),
using {data.env.compiler}. Serial runs use {data.env.threads} thread. LM uses
nalgebra {data.versions.nalgebra}; the other cases use `Vec<f64>`, except for
gomez's bundled `nalgebra::DVector<f64>`. Native NLopt is accessed through
the Rust `nlopt` bindings, version {data.versions.nlopt}.

Each timestamp is the median at the corresponding sample over
{data.repetitions} repetitions of the same deterministic run. Only the timing
varies. NLopt exposes objective callbacks rather than an iteration hook, so
its curves record best-so-far function evaluations. The other gradient-descent,
Nelder–Mead, L-BFGS, and NEWUOA curves are sampled by their driving loops or
observers. Instrumentation is included in the timings, and the sampling units
differ.

LM and COBYLA include construction, initialization, callbacks, and solver
teardown. Timing excludes fixture preparation and independent solution checks.
Each of these comparisons warms up once before its repeated runs. Endpoints
include work after the last evaluation. COBYLA feasibility is reconstructed
afterward from recorded trial points. Independent accuracy checks do not
relabel a termination status as convergence.

Objective gaps are floored at $10^{-16}$ for display, including small negative
gaps permitted by COBYLA's feasibility tolerance. Absolute times are specific to
this machine. The measurements use source revision
<code>{data.env.revision.slice(0, 7)}</code>{revisionNote}.

From the repository root, run the harness and collect its output:

```sh
mkdir -p target
cargo run -p competitor-bench --release --bin trace > target/competitor-traces.json
pnpm --dir web collect:competitors
```

The [collection script](https://github.com/jolars/basin/blob/main/web/scripts/collect-competitors.ts)
adds machine and version metadata to the
[native trace harness](https://github.com/jolars/basin/blob/main/crates/competitor-bench/src/bin/trace.rs)
output. The harness defines the problems and solver settings; the committed
results supply the figures on this page.
